I'll pursue the other potential segmentation methods you suggested. One quick follow up to make sure I'm thinking about this correctly... The majority of ducks in my sample displayed similar variograms to the one above (reach asymptote relatively rapidly and show daily periodicity related to a combination of foraging vs roosting behavior and transmitter program). Am I correct in thinking that a rapid achievement of asymptote provides support for the argument that there is a relaitvely short prospecting/dispersal period before individuals establish a home range? So home ranges are established quickly when an individual arrives to the wintering area and, therefore, data segmentation into pre- and post- range residency establishment is not really valid? (with additional support from other metrics such as daily dispersal and locality over time).

Much appreciated!

Tori

